{"name":"Aromatic Lemon","hex":"#f2ff26","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aromatic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Fragrant or spicy.","Having a closed ring of alternate single and double bonds with delocalized electrons."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fragrant plant or spice added to a dish to flavour it, especially the vegetables that serve as the base flavors in a more complex dish.","A plant grown for fragrant properties."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aromatic"},{"term":"lemon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A yellowish citrus fruit.","A semitropical evergreen tree, Citrus limon, that bears such fruits."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Containing or having the flavour/flavor and/or scent of lemons.","Of the pale yellow colour/color of lemons."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lemon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aromaticity","blocks":[{"text":"In organic chemistry, aromaticity is a chemical property describing the way in which a conjugated ring of unsaturated bonds, lone pairs, or empty orbitals exhibits a stabilization stronger than would be expected from conjugation alone. The earliest use of the term was in an article by August Wilhelm von Hofmann in 1855. There is no general relationship between aromaticity as a chemical property and the olfactory properties of such compounds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Benzene_resonance_structures.png/330px-Benzene_resonance_structures.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aromaticity"},{"title":"Lemon","blocks":[{"text":"The lemon is a species of small evergreen tree in the Citrus genus of the flowering plant family Rutaceae. A true lemon is a hybrid of the citron and the bitter orange. Its origins are uncertain, but some evidence suggests lemons originated during the 1st millennium BC in what is now northeastern India. Some other citrus fruits are called lemon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/P1030323.JPG/330px-P1030323.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lemon"},{"title":"Lemon (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lemon or lemon-color is a vivid yellow color characteristic of the lemon fruit. Shades of \"lemon\" may vary significantly from the fruit's actual color, including fluorescent tones and creamy hues reflective of lemon pies and confections."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Lemon_-_whole_and_split.jpg/330px-Lemon_-_whole_and_split.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lemon_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Benzene-orbitals.png/500px-Benzene-orbitals.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Benzene-orbitals.png/500px-Benzene-orbitals.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABenzene-orbitals.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Historic_Benzene_Formulae_Kekul%C3%A9_%28original%29.png/500px-Historic_Benzene_Formulae_Kekul%C3%A9_%28original%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Historic_Benzene_Formulae_Kekul%C3%A9_%28original%29.png/500px-Historic_Benzene_Formulae_Kekul%C3%A9_%28original%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Historic benzene formulae as proposed by Kekulé.[5]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHistoric_Benzene_Formulae_Kekul%C3%A9_(original).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/t